\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The parity barrier and three structural bypasses. (a) Fixed-scale quantised inference (FSQI) with fixed latency cannot compute global parity across arbitrarily large boards. Here fixed latency means a bounded number of sequential computation steps, independent of board size. (b) Two frames expose $\Delta $ for restoration when the predecessor has nim-sum zero. (c) Distinct policy heads recover complementary information through complete rollouts. (d) Persistent recurrence accumulates the nim-sum row by row and retains summaries across positions. Each update has constant depth; the full scan has depth growing with $N$.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:barrier-blueprint}{{1}{3}{The parity barrier and three structural bypasses. (a) Fixed-scale quantised inference (FSQI) with fixed latency cannot compute global parity across arbitrarily large boards. Here fixed latency means a bounded number of sequential computation steps, independent of board size. (b) Two frames expose $\Delta $ for restoration when the predecessor has nim-sum zero. (c) Distinct policy heads recover complementary information through complete rollouts. (d) Persistent recurrence accumulates the nim-sum row by row and retains summaries across positions. Each update has constant depth; the full scan has depth growing with $N$}{figure.1}{}}
